#!/bin/sh
# M5 appliance autostart template (ARCHITECTURE.md §8/§9,
# docs/development/appliance-recovery.md). Shipped at
# /usr/share/aa-headunit/labwc-autostart; `postinst` copies it to
# ~/.config/labwc/autostart for whichever real account is already
# running the desktop at install time (not a dedicated login account —
# this app installs onto an existing PiOS desktop and runs as whoever's
# already using it, overwritten on every install/upgrade since it's
# package-managed content, not something meant to be hand-edited).
#
# Supersedes two earlier designs: `aa-headunit-kiosk@.service` (a
# separately-managed `cage` session on a spare VT, dropped because the
# operator wants a real desktop underneath the kiosk app, not a bare
# compositor), and a dedicated `aa-headunit` *login* account (dropped
# because this is a program installed onto someone's own already-set-up
# Pi, not appliance hardware with its own dedicated account — the
# installing user's own account should autologin, matching how they
# already use the machine). `aa-headunit` remains only as a permissions
# group anchor (`/etc/aa-headunit`, `/var/lib/aa-headunit`, USB/GPIO
# access), not a session identity.
#
# Real-hardware finding (2026-08-19): `/usr/bin/labwc-pi` always execs
# `labwc -m` ("merge-config" — confirmed via `labwc --help`: "Merge user
# config files/theme in all XDG Base Dirs"), which runs BOTH the system
# default (`/etc/xdg/labwc/autostart`) AND this per-user file — it does
# NOT replace one with the other the way plain (non-`-m`) labwc's own
# docs describe. An earlier version of this file assumed the opposite
# and re-launched the panel/file-manager/kanshi/xdg-autostart itself "to
# avoid losing them" — that was never actually at risk, and instead
# caused every one of them to run twice (confirmed: two `wf-panel-pi`
# processes, a visibly doubled taskbar, and periodic white/blank
# flashes as both instances fought to composite the same outputs). This
# file must contain ONLY what the system default does not already do —
# the app launch below.
#
# Fails open to the plain desktop rather than retrying a broken
# kiosk session on every boot: `launch-on-boot-enabled` checks the
# operator's own persisted preference (Display settings page) — **off
# by default** (2026-08-19, deliberate operator decision: a real-hardware
# trial the same day found "return to desktop" can hang the whole
# compositor with no recourse but a physical reboot; an unattended
# appliance that can silently hang on boot with no way to intervene is
# worse than one that boots to a plain, always-usable desktop — see
# `docs/development/appliance-recovery.md`). Turning this on is an
# explicit opt-in once that hang is confirmed fixed on real hardware.
# Preflight (ARCHITECTURE.md §9 — display, touch input, writable state
# dir, USB,
# audio) gates whether the fullscreen app is even attempted, exactly
# like the old unit's `Requires=aa-headunit-preflight.service` did, just
# as a plain in-script check instead of a separate systemd dependency
# now that there is no longer a discrete unit for it to gate. The
# desktop shortcut (`aa-headunit.desktop`, `~/Desktop` and the
# application menu) still launches the app manually regardless of the
# "launch on boot" setting.
if /usr/bin/aa-headunit-diagnostics launch-on-boot-enabled \
    && /usr/bin/aa-headunit-diagnostics preflight; then
    exec /usr/bin/aa-headunit-diagnostics usb kiosk --allow-live-aap
fi
